# Audit results and detailed explanations, part F (q301-q360). Format: see explain_a.py.
E = {}
def e(qid, stem, exp, opts=None, q=None):
    E[qid] = dict(stem=stem, exp=exp, opts=opts, q=q)

e("q301", "Which is a major way small businesses help a market", "Small businesses make up about 99.9% of U.S. businesses and employ nearly half of private-sector workers (SBA). They create jobs, bring new ideas to market and compete with larger firms, which keeps prices and quality in check. They don't set interest rates or prevent inflation.")
e("q302", "Which activity is a main responsibility of human resources", "Human resources management handles the people side of a business: recruiting, hiring, training, pay and benefits, performance reviews and employee relations. Approving loans is a banking function, managing investments is finance and designing ads is marketing.")
e("q303", "An HR manager learns that two candidates are equally qualified", "Choosing a candidate because she went to the same college is a personal bias, not a job-related reason. Ethical, fair hiring uses consistent criteria tied to the job. Favoritism like this can also create a less diverse workforce and legal risk.")
e("q304", "A company uses software that tracks employee hours", "A human resource information system (HRIS) puts payroll, hours, benefits and performance records in one system, automating paperwork and improving accuracy. It's a technology tool for HR. CRM software manages customers, not employees.")
e("q305", "Which is a main purpose of new-employee orientation", "Orientation introduces new hires to the company's policies, culture, people and their own role, so they feel welcome and become productive faster. Good orientation lowers early turnover. It doesn't evaluate firing, set lifetime pay or replace job training.")
e("q306", "Which is the best description of marketing", "Marketing is the process of creating, communicating and delivering value to customers. It includes research, product decisions, pricing, distribution, promotion and selling. Advertising is just one part, and manufacturing and record-keeping belong to other functions.")
e("q307", "A bank studies which age groups use its mobile app", "Marketing-information management gathers and analyzes data about customers and markets, such as who uses the app, to guide marketing decisions. Channel management deals with distribution, pricing sets prices and selling is direct communication with customers.")
e("q308", "Which employee action best helps a bank achieve its goal", "Following up to confirm a customer's problem was solved shows care and directly raises satisfaction. Rushing customers, avoiding difficult ones or pushing unneeded products all hurt satisfaction, even if they seem efficient in the moment.")
e("q309", "After a bank adds Saturday hours, new account openings", "Businesses measure how their decisions affect results. Adding Saturday hours (an action) followed by a 15% rise in account openings (a result) shows a connection between them, which tells the bank the change worked. It isn't an external factor because the bank chose it.")
e("q310", "The management function of setting goals and deciding", "Planning sets goals and decides how to reach them: strategies, budgets and timelines. It comes first. Organizing arranges people and resources, staffing hires and trains people and controlling compares results with the plan.")
e("q311", "Buying insurance to cover possible losses is which", "Buying insurance transfers the financial risk of a loss to the insurance company in exchange for a premium. The four main strategies are avoid (don't do it), reduce or control (lower the chance or impact), transfer (insurance or contracts) and retain (accept it and pay losses yourself).")
e("q312", "A bank decides not to offer cryptocurrency trading", "Risk avoidance means choosing not to take part in a risky activity at all. By not offering crypto trading, the bank removes that risk entirely, but also gives up any potential revenue. Transferring would mean insuring it, and retaining would mean accepting it.")
e("q313", "When conducting a risk assessment for a company event", "After identifying risks, the next step is to assess each one: how likely is it, and how severe would the impact be? Ranking risks on a likelihood-times-impact matrix shows which deserve attention and money first. Canceling everything or insuring every risk isn't practical.")
e("q314", "Which is an external factor that can affect how a bank", "External factors come from outside the organization, such as the economy, laws, competitors and technology. Federal Reserve interest-rate changes directly affect what a bank pays depositors and charges borrowers. Dress codes, lobby layout and leadership style are internal.")
e("q315", "Why should a young worker start investing for retirement", "Starting early gives compound growth more years to work, as earnings earn their own returns. $200 a month from 22 to 65 at 8% grows to about $895,000, but starting at 32 gives about $387,000. No investment is guaranteed, and retirement accounts have no age cutoff.")
e("q316", "Which is the main reason to keep an emergency fund", "An emergency fund must be available immediately and stable in value. Stocks can fall 20% or more right when you lose a job or face a big bill, forcing you to sell at a loss. An insured savings account keeps the money liquid and safe; stocks are for long-term goals.")
e("q317", "Setting up an automatic transfer from each paycheck", "Paying yourself first means saving before you pay bills or spend, usually by automatic transfer on payday. Because the money moves automatically, saving happens without relying on willpower. Dollar-cost averaging is investing a fixed amount on a schedule, a related idea.")
e("q318", "Investing the same dollar amount in a fund every month", "Dollar-cost averaging invests a fixed dollar amount on a regular schedule regardless of price. The same $300 buys more shares when prices are low and fewer when they're high, and it removes the temptation to time the market. Market timing tries to buy low and sell high, which rarely works.")
e("q319", "Which investment pools money from many investors and tracks", "An index fund, a mutual fund or ETF, pools investors' money and holds the stocks in an index such as the S&P 500. It offers instant diversification across hundreds of companies at very low fees, often under 0.10% a year. CDs and bonds are single debt investments.")
e("q320", "Which investment generally has the highest risk and the highest", "Stocks are ownership in companies, so their value rises and falls with profits and the economy. That makes them the most volatile of these choices, but over long periods they've earned the most, about 10% a year for the S&P 500 since 1928. CDs, T-bills and savings are safer but earn less.")
e("q321", "When you buy a bond, you are", "A bond is a loan from the investor to a government or company. The issuer pays interest (the coupon) and repays the face value at maturity. Bondholders are creditors, not owners; buying stock makes you an owner.")
e("q322", "Spreading money across stocks, bonds and cash", "Diversification spreads money across different investments that don't all move together, so a loss in one is cushioned by others. It lowers risk without giving up much return. Leverage is borrowing to invest, which increases risk.")
e("q323", "A fund that automatically shifts from mostly stocks", "A target-date fund is named for a retirement year, such as 2065, and automatically shifts from mostly stocks to more bonds as that year approaches, becoming more conservative over time. It's a simple one-fund choice and a common 401(k) default.")
e("q324", "A real estate investment trust (REIT) lets an investor", "A REIT owns income-producing properties, like apartments, offices, warehouses or malls, and sells shares to investors, who get a slice of the rental income. REITs must pay out at least 90% of taxable income as dividends. They still carry market risk.")
e("q325", "Saving for a house down payment in four years", "Financial goals are grouped by time: short-term (under about a year), medium-term (about 1 to 5 years) and long-term (more than 5 years). A down payment in four years is medium-term, so the money belongs in safer choices like high-yield savings, CDs or Treasuries, not mostly stocks.")
e("q326", "Using the Rule of 72, about how long will it take money", "The Rule of 72 estimates doubling time: divide 72 by the annual return. 72 / 8 = 9 years. It's a quick way to show clients the power of compounding, and why moving from a 4% to an 8% return can cut doubling time from 18 to 9 years.")
e("q327", "You deposit $5,000 at 4% interest compounded annually", "Compound interest earns interest on interest. Year 1: $5,000 x 1.04 = $5,200. Year 2: $5,200 x 1.04 = $5,408. Simple interest would give $5,400; the extra $8 is interest earned on year 1's $200 of interest.")
e("q328", "Simple interest on $3,000 at 5% per year", "Simple interest = principal x rate x time = $3,000 x 0.05 x 3 = $450. Simple interest is calculated only on the original principal, never on interest already earned. $3,450 would be the total balance, not the interest.")
e("q329", "An employer matches 100% of employee 401(k) contributions", "The employee contributes 4% of $50,000 = $2,000. A 100% match up to 4% means the employer adds the same $2,000, for $4,000 total a year. That match is an instant 100% return, which is why getting the full match is usually step one in any savings plan.")
e("q330", "What is the main tax difference between a Roth IRA", "With a Roth IRA you contribute money that's already been taxed, and qualified withdrawals in retirement, including all the growth, are tax-free. A traditional IRA may give a deduction now, but withdrawals are taxed later. Both can hold stocks.")
e("q331", "Which account offers tax-free growth when the money", "A 529 plan is a state-sponsored education savings account. Earnings grow free of federal tax, and withdrawals are tax-free when used for qualified education expenses such as tuition, books, and room and board, plus K-12 tuition and some apprenticeships. Many states add a tax deduction.")
e("q332", "A legal document that states how a person's property", "A will directs who receives your property after death, names an executor to carry it out and can name a guardian for minor children. A power of attorney lets someone act for you while you're alive, and it ends at death. A deed transfers title to real estate.")
e("q333", "Kendra has $18,000 in assets and owes $11,000", "Net worth = assets - liabilities = $18,000 - $11,000 = $7,000. It's a snapshot of financial position: what you'd have if you sold everything and paid every debt. Tracking it over time shows whether you're making progress.")
e("q334", "Under the 50/30/20 budgeting guideline", "The 50/30/20 guideline splits after-tax income into 50% for needs (housing, food, insurance), 30% for wants (dining out, entertainment) and 20% for savings and extra debt payments. It's a simple starting framework that can be adjusted.")
e("q335", "On a pay stub, the amount left after taxes", "Net pay, or take-home pay, is what's left after taxes (federal, state, Social Security, Medicare) and other deductions (health insurance, 401(k)) are subtracted from gross pay. Gross pay is total earnings before deductions, and budgets should be built on net pay.")
e("q336", "Which tax is withheld from paychecks to fund Social", "FICA (Federal Insurance Contributions Act) taxes fund Social Security (6.2% of wages up to the wage base) and Medicare (1.45% of all wages). Employers pay a matching amount. Sales, property and excise taxes aren't withheld from paychecks.")
e("q337", "Which habit does the most to help a person build a good", "Payment history is the largest factor in a FICO score, about 35%, so paying every bill on time does the most for your score. Opening many cards at once adds hard inquiries, maxing out cards raises utilization, and closing your oldest card shortens your credit history.")
e("q338", "A credit card has a $2,000 limit and a $1,500 balance", "Credit utilization = balance / credit limit = $1,500 / $2,000 = 0.75, or 75%. High utilization signals heavy reliance on credit and lowers your score. Keeping it under 30%, and ideally under 10%, helps.")
e("q339", "How can consumers check their credit reports", "AnnualCreditReport.com is the only official site, authorized by federal law, for free credit reports from Equifax, Experian and TransUnion, now available weekly. Credit repair companies charge for what you can do free, and checking your own report never hurts your score.")
e("q340", "A $1,000 loan for one year costs $120 in interest", "The finance charge is the total cost of credit in dollars, including interest and fees: $120 + $30 = $150. The Truth in Lending Act requires lenders to disclose it along with the APR. $1,150 would be the total repaid, not the cost of borrowing.")
e("q341", "Paying off debts from the highest interest rate", "The avalanche method makes minimum payments on all debts and puts every extra dollar toward the highest-interest debt first, which saves the most interest overall. The snowball method pays the smallest balance first for quick motivational wins.")
e("q342", "Which action best protects against identity theft", "A credit freeze at Equifax, Experian and TransUnion stops anyone from opening new credit in your name. It's free, doesn't affect your score and can be lifted temporarily when you apply for credit. Reusing passwords, carrying your Social Security card or posting account numbers all increase risk.")
e("q343", "The Truth in Lending Act requires lenders to", "The Truth in Lending Act (1968) requires lenders to clearly disclose the cost of credit, including the APR and total finance charge, before you sign, so you can compare offers. It doesn't require the lowest rate, approval of every application or interest-free loans.")
e("q344", "Which document will a lender most likely request", "Lenders need to verify capacity, your ability to repay, so they ask for proof of income such as recent pay stubs, W-2s or tax returns, and they check your credit report. School transcripts, medical records and lists of friends aren't relevant to repaying a car loan.")
e("q345", "A couple with a new baby depends on both of their incomes", "When a family depends on both incomes, losing either parent would cause a serious financial loss. Life insurance on both parents, often term life of 10 to 12 times income, replaces that income so the child is protected. A new baby is a key life event that triggers an insurance review.")
e("q346", "Which insurance replaces part of your income if an illness", "Disability insurance pays a portion of your income, often 60% to 70%, if illness or injury keeps you from working. About 1 in 4 of today's 20-year-olds will become disabled before retirement age (SSA). Liability insurance covers harm to others, and life insurance pays at death.")
e("q347", "An inexpensive policy that adds extra liability protection", "Umbrella insurance adds liability coverage, often $1 million or more, above the limits of your home and auto policies, protecting against large lawsuits. It usually costs a few hundred dollars a year, making it an inexpensive way to protect your assets.")
e("q348", "The amount a policyholder must pay out of pocket", "The deductible is what you pay out of pocket on a claim before insurance pays the rest. With a $1,000 deductible and a $5,000 loss, you pay $1,000 and the insurer pays $4,000. The premium is the regular price of the policy, and a beneficiary receives a life insurance payout.")
e("q349", "Choosing a higher deductible on an insurance policy", "With a higher deductible you pay more of each loss yourself, so the insurer's expected costs fall and it charges a lower premium. It's a good trade-off when your emergency fund can cover the higher deductible.")
e("q350", "Which type of life insurance lasts your whole life", "Whole (permanent) life insurance covers you for life and builds a cash value you can borrow against. Because of that, its premiums are much higher than term life for the same death benefit. Term covers a set period with no cash value.")
e("q351", "The FDIC insures deposits at member banks up to", "FDIC insurance covers $250,000 per depositor, per insured bank, per ownership category, such as individual, joint or retirement accounts, so careful titling can insure more at one bank. The NCUA gives the same coverage at credit unions. No depositor has lost insured funds since 1934.")
e("q352", "Which statement about credit unions compared with banks", "Credit unions are not-for-profit cooperatives owned by their members, so earnings go back to members as lower loan rates, higher savings rates and fewer fees. Deposits are insured up to $250,000 by the NCUA, and they make loans like banks do.")
e("q353", "Which organization protects customers' securities", "SIPC (Securities Investor Protection Corporation) protects customers if a brokerage firm fails, up to $500,000, including $250,000 in cash. It doesn't cover losses from falling markets. The FDIC insures bank deposits, the SEC regulates markets and FINRA oversees brokers.")
e("q354", "A company sells new shares of stock to the public", "When a company sells new shares to the public for the first time, it's an initial public offering in the primary market, and the money goes to the company. Afterward, investors trade those shares with each other in the secondary market.")
e("q355", "Which market is used for long-term borrowing and investing", "The capital market handles long-term funds through stocks and bonds that last longer than a year, which pay for things like factories and infrastructure. The money market handles short-term debt of a year or less, such as T-bills and commercial paper.")
e("q356", "A U.S. company exchanges dollars for euros", "Currencies are bought and sold in the foreign exchange (forex) market, the largest financial market in the world at about $9.6 trillion a day (BIS, 2025). Paying a German supplier in euros requires converting dollars there. Stocks and bonds trade in other markets.")
e("q357", "A long period in which stock prices fall 20% or more", "A bear market is a decline of 20% or more from a recent peak, often lasting months. A correction is a smaller drop of about 10%, a bull market is a long rise and a rally is a short-term rise.")
e("q358", "When interest rates rise, what generally happens to the prices", "When rates rise, new bonds pay higher interest, so older bonds with lower fixed rates are less attractive. Their prices fall until their yield matches the market. This inverse relationship caused bond funds to drop about 13% in 2022.")
e("q359", "An investor buys shares of a Japanese company", "An ADR's value is tied to the Japanese company's shares, priced in yen. If the yen weakens against the dollar, the investment is worth fewer dollars even if the stock price in yen is unchanged. That's currency (exchange-rate) risk, the main added risk of foreign investing.")
e("q360", "The Gramm-Leach-Bliley Act of 1999 allowed", "The Gramm-Leach-Bliley Act repealed parts of the Glass-Steagall Act, allowing banks, securities firms and insurance companies to combine under one holding company. That enabled financial convergence, like Citigroup. It also added privacy and data-protection rules. The FDIC was created in 1933.")
